.SS Private Keys in otpme-agent
These three are what the key script uses.
They are listed here because a human may want to look, not because a human has to use them.
.PP
otpme-agent holds the private keys of the login session in two shapes: as the login brought them, wrapped with the key password of the user, and unwrapped, as the key script left them after it was first asked to use one.
The unwrapped one lives for \fBkey_cache_time\fR of the user.
Both are only handed to the login session they belong to.
See \fBenable_key_caching\fR in
.BR otpme\-user (1).
.TP
.B get_private_key \fB\-\-role\fR \fIsign\fR|\fIencrypt\fR \fR[\fB\-\-decrypted\fR]
Write a private key of the logged in user to stdout.
Without \fB\-\-decrypted\fR the wrapped one, with it the unwrapped one.
It fails when the agent holds none, which is the normal answer before the key script has been asked to use the key, and after the cache time ran out.
.TP
.B add_private_key \fB\-\-role\fR \fIsign\fR|\fIencrypt\fR \fR[\fB\-\-decrypted\fR]
Give the agent a private key, read from stdin.
\fB\-\-decrypted\fR for the unwrapped one, which is then kept for the cache time.
.br
From stdin, because a private key on a command line is a private key in the process list.
.TP
.B del_private_key \fB\-\-role\fR \fIsign\fR|\fIencrypt\fR
Take a private key away from the agent, both shapes of it.
The next use asks for the key password again.
This is how to drop a cached key without logging out.
